data.error-types#Error Types:
An error is a class that implements the builtin IError interface:
interface IError {
    method code() i32;
    method message() String;
}
Define your own by implementing both methods. Because an error is an ordinary
class, it can carry whatever fields and extra methods you need:
class ParseError implements IError {
    var line_: i32;
    init(line: i32) { this.line_ = line; }
    method code() i32 { return 1; }
    method message() String { return String("bad input"); }
    method line() i32 { return this.line_; }
}
The standard library (using std;) provides Error(code, message) plus a set
of ready-made ones: EmptyError, NotFoundError, IndexOutOfBoundsError,
DivisionByZeroError, OverflowError, InvalidArgumentError, IOError,
AllocationError and ConversionError. Error accepts its message as a literal or as a String
built at runtime, and keeps its own copy.

data.throwing-functions#Throwing Functions:
A function that can fail declares it with the throws IError suffix after its
return type, and raises an error with throw. throw takes an error object,
never a bare number:
function divide(a: i32, b: i32) i32 throws IError {
    if (b == 0) { throw DivisionByZeroError(); }
    return a / b;
}
A constructor can fail too: it has no return type, so the suffix stands
alone — init(args) throws IError { }.A function without the suffix cannot throw. Calling a throwing function is
only allowed from inside a try block or from another throwing function, so
an error either gets handled or is visibly passed up the signature chain — it
can never escape unnoticed:
// Propagates: no try needed, the error keeps unwinding to the caller.
function half_of_quotient(a: i32, b: i32) i32 throws IError {
    return divide(a, b) / 2;
}

data.handling-errors-with-trycatch#Handling E:
try is block-form only. Each catch names a binding and its type, and the
clauses are tested in order against the concrete type of the thrown error:
function main() i32 {
    try {
        var r = divide(10, 0);
        return r;
    } catch (e: DivisionByZeroError) {
        return -1;                 // this exact error type
    } catch (e: IError) {
        return e.code();           // anything else
    }
}
catch (e: IError) matches every error; a clause after it is unreachable
and rejected.
catch (e: SomeError) matches only that class. The binding is the real
object, so methods unique to that class (like line() above) are callable.
When no clause matches, the error keeps unwinding to the enclosing try or
caller.
Inside a catch clause, e.code() and e.message() dispatch to the thrown
class. message() returns an owned String that stays valid after the
handler ends.
Inside a throwing function, integer division and modulo by zero throw
instead of crashing; catch (e: IError) catches them.

data.manifests#Manifests:
Sun programs and libraries declare their dependencies using a manifest block, written in
the source file itself:
manifest {
    source_files: ["utils.sun", "math.sun"]  // All source files except for the one containing this manifest block
    libraries: [
        "stdlib.moon",                       // Found via SUN_PATH
        {                                    // Fetched once, then cached
            url: "https://example.com/libs/mylib.moon",
            hash: "" // Optional
        }
    ]
    target: { // Target-specific files
        linux: {
            source_files: ["platform_linux.sun"]
            archives: ["vendor/libfoo-linux.a"]
        }
        macos: {
            source_files: ["platform_macos.sun"]
            archives: ["vendor/libfoo-macos.a"]
        }
    }
}
Paths are resolved relative to the source file, then via SUN_PATH. A library
given as a url is downloaded with curl on first use and reused from the
cache afterwards; the optional hash is the bundle's SHA-256, and a file that
does not match it is rejected. A target block takes the same sections as the
manifest itself and applies only when compiling for that operating system —
the manifest never picks the target, --target (or the host) does. archives
are static C libraries carried inside a bundle built with --emit-moon, so
whoever imports it links them without needing -l flags.

data.moons#Moons:
Moons (.moon files) are precompiled library bundles containing compiled code and type information. Create a moon from any .sun file containing a manifest:
public module mylib {
    public function helper() i32 { return 42; }
}
manifest {
    source_files: ["utils.sun", "math.sun"]
}
sun --emit-moon -o mylib.moon mylib.sun
A moon is linked into whatever imports it, so a compiled program carries the
moons it uses — nothing to install beside the binary, and nothing to load at
run time.C libraries follow the same preference: sun -c links statically by default,
preferring musl where it is installed, and produces one self-contained
executable. Dynamic linkage is still available — --dynamic links against
shared objects, which vendor SDKs shipped only as .so files need, and macOS
targets are always dynamic because Apple ships no static libSystem. See
C FFI for the details.

